title: Storage
kind: overview
aliases: store lmdb kv kvstore
summary: Each repository's metadata and history live in a key-value store (LMDB) on disk; nothing proportional to the repository stays in memory.

Each repository has its own ''store'': one LMDB database (through the `heed` crate)
in `.metafolder/internal/kv/`, holding its metarecords, their fields and its
operation log ([[Repository layout on disk]]). Only one daemon at a time can have it
open ([[The repository lock]]).

!! Two kinds of data

|!Kind |!What |!If it is wrong |
|''primary data'' |the metarecords, their field rows, the operation log and HEAD — what was written |data is lost: the defence is [[Backups and restore]] |
|''indexes'' — in the code, the ''derived key spaces'' |dense ids, bitmaps, value partitions, the forest's bitmaps, trigrams, postings — computed from the primary data to answer queries |queries answer wrongly, nothing is lost: `mf repo reindex` rebuilds them ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]) |

Both are written in the same transaction by every write, so they agree by
construction ([[Writes and atomicity]]).

!! Memory

The store is ''memory-mapped'': opening it reads nothing, the operating system's page
cache keeps the pages in use and drops the others, and the daemon holds nothing whose
size grows with the repository. So a repository of any size works on an ordinary
machine — the worst case is being slower, never running out of memory. A repository
that fits in memory ends up in the page cache on its own, or at once, and kept there,
when loaded in memory ([[Holding a repository in memory]]).

Queries are answered from the indexes directly, one snapshot per request: there is
no index to build at load and no SQL (SQLite was dropped in September 2026 —
[[How the storage backend was built]]). How a query uses them: [[Query engine]].

!! Where to start

* ''Using a repository'': [[Backups and restore]], [[Checking and reindexing a repository]], [[Holding a repository in memory]].
* ''Working on the store'': [[The storage boundary]] (the traits the rest of the daemon speaks), then [[Store tables]] (what is stored, under which keys), then the notes on each index: [[The forest in the store]], [[Text search in the store]], [[Sorting and postings]].
* ''Before changing it'': [[Storage safety]] and [[Storage pitfalls]]; a change to what is stored needs its migration ([[Persisted format changes]]).

